cal impact  and the antimicrobial stewardship and  three pulmonologists who were part of  the selection process  of patients dr rodrigo cavallazi  dr alan ramirez and dr mohammad  and last but not least dr forrest arnold  infectious diseases  who also was in the process of selecting  patients  for bals and entering into the study  so with that i will thank you and  would be available for any questions  once the webinar is completed  i will now turn the program over to dr  hoffman  thank you dr schneider my name is  wes hoffman i and i'm an infectious  disease pharmacist at the university of  louisville health system  today i'll be going over the second  portion of this discussion which is  uh the clinical utility of the univarial  lower respiratory tract panel  specifically from an antimicrobial  stewardship standpoint  so as probably many of us are familiar  antimicrobial stewardship  especially in an intensive care unit  setting is number one critically  important  and number two an area of potentially  very high impact  the point prevalence of antimicrobial  use in the icu can be as high as 70  which was first identified in 1995 but  since then has  likely stayed the same or possibly even  gotten  worse in some of those settings  unfortunately only about 45 percent  of patients had a culture confirmation  of the edli  etiologic organism  and with patients in the icu as many of  us are familiar much  of the antimicrobial use involves very  broad spectrum antibiotics things like  carbopenems  vancomycin lynasolid etc  now there are some ways to combat this  effective stewardship methods  and education are going to be one key  component  to helping battle the overuse of  antimicrobials in icu settings  but that's likely likely going to be  used  in combination with rapid diagnostics  to help reduce overall how many  antibiotics are used overall how many  toxic medications are used and to  decrease the amount of  antimicrobials that are used overall  now ideally with the use of rapid  diagnostics you're going to  want some technology that has favorable  sensitivity specificity  and a negative predictive value to help  make clinical decisions that are  appropriate  in the setting of critically ill  patients  despite identification of organisms via  culture  rapid diagnostics can help decrease  unnecessary broad spectrum use by  getting you answers potential mechanisms  resistance that would otherwise take  potentially anywhere from two to four  days until you get final susceptibility  results which is how long patients may  often have to remain on broad spectrum  antimicrobials  before you can de-escalate to what may  end up being a pan-susceptible organism  the other factor that comes into play  here is is for some idsa guidelines  especially in the setting of pneumonia  combination therapy is often recommended  in things like ventilator associated  pneumonia  oftentimes with aminoglycosides given  the high amount of resistance that can  be found in icus  if you have to continue these  combination therapies that are  exceptionally broad spectrum that  increases the risk of adverse events  and potential development of resistance  later on  now the study that we did here with  univero which  to our knowledge is the first time that  this has been done prospectively  was to take patients who were in icu  care who had a bronchial v or lavage  done for diagnostic purposes  and it was evaluated with the univero  lower respiratory tract panel  this was done in addition to standard  culture and susceptibility testing which  is always going to be a component  done in conjunction with the universe  now the results from the univaro were  communicated to the primary team  in this study when the results were  available and  they were also reported to the  infectious disease pharmacist  who's a member of the antimicrobial  stewardship team  we had 15 patients included in the  clinical evaluation  of this study and  as we can note here de-escalation  occurred in 33  of patients now this was sometimes just  de-escalation of mrsa therapy  sometimes just the escalation of  anti-pseudomonal therapy  and at times it was a combination of  de-escalating from mrsa and  anti-pseudomonal therapy  percent of patients ended up having an  escalation occur one of these cases  involving a carbopentamise producer  another case involving esbl  times when we were not adequately  covering what the patient ended up  having identified and subsequently  confirmed in culture  overall a little bit over half of  patients had  some change made with the universe lower  respiratory tract panel  and oftentimes this was done faster than  conventional methods  so the results that were reported as i  mentioned earlier were reported the  infectious disease pharmacist who then  discussed with the team  at the earliest potential opportunity  the  clinical ramifications of what those  results  demonstrated and as i said earlier the  changes in therapy  often occurred significantly faster than  with conventional methods so  with our group of patients here on  average it was a 21-hour faster  change in therapy with univera results  and this ranged anywhere from  zero hours meaning we couldn't really  make any change  all the way up to a little under two  days sooner  now the little asterisk here is that  this is compared to when  susceptibilities resulted  because this is not a head-to-head trial  of doing  some patients with the univero panel and  some patients without there's no way we  can really tell how soon they would have  changed  based on the susceptibilities reported  so this is  perhaps a more conservative estimate  knowing full well that when  susceptibilities are reported oftentimes  those are not seen for anywhere from an  hour to until potentially the next day  now when it comes to how to utilize  univero this is kind of the method that  we ended up following  and the first two are relatively easy to  do  for for anybody involved so escalation  obviously if you identify something that  you're currently not covering say  somebody is on ceftrax and you identify  pseudomonas that's an  easy use of the unit vero to get them on  appropriate therapy right away  if you have them on cefepime and you  identify e coli  with a third generation cephalosporin  resistance gene such as ctxm which is  identified by univero  that would allow you to rapidly escalate  someone to a carbopenum  uh and it's a fairly simple task to do  the other aspect which is particularly  easy to use  is the de-escalation of mrsa therapy now  largely in icu settings vancomycin or  nasal it is really going to be utilized  almost exclusively for mrsa given that  the negative predictive value of  universe for staph aureus is about 97  and knowing that potentially half of  those  can be mssa that really gives you  a pretty solid indication that mrsa is  not present and causing  infection in this patient at least from  a respiratory standpoint  now of course univiro doesn't take the  place of  something like positive blood cultures  in the setting of endocarditis but  this is to be used for the clinical  diagnosis of pneumonia  and by being able to de-escalate from  mrsa therapy  there's really three major benefits to  that number one  you're not using that drug that's that's  helping that patient  that's reducing the cost of the drug  it's mitigating the potential for  adverse effects such as vancomycin  induced nephrotoxicity which is  of course very well described and lastly  it's also a reduction in cost  of managing vancomycin  in these settings so that requires  additional blood draws  to get levels done sometimes two if your  institution is doing area under the  curve monitoring and doing a two-level  evaluation  it also saves pharmacists time who  otherwise have to manage  both the levels and the calculations and  the changes in doses  of vancomycin so those are the two easy  ones escalation  overall and de-escalation of