ng like berkeled area  which is not identified by univero  but if univero is negative and that  patient has a history of birkeldera  you're probably not going to de-escalate  in that sitting  that's a overall probably a significant  minority of patients that will have  something like this going on in the icu  so this can still be useful you just  always have to keep those patient  specific factors in mind  the other thing to keep in mind  especially for stewardship programs is  that willingness to de-escalate may  depend  on the patient's clinical status and the  comfort level of the primary team i'm  sure we're all familiar with  patients who are very critically ill  and possibly on for pressers and  quite honestly fear drives a lot of  antimicrobial use especially in icus  primary teams may not necessarily want  to rock the boat if a patient has  improved ever so slightly on combination  therapy  even though logically they may trust the  results of this  it may be difficult to in real time  de-escalate  drastically in this setting they may  want to wait till the next day and  that's understandable  i'm sure many of us have been burned by  missing an organism from time to time  but with that being said the the very  nice thing  about univero is that pseudomonas and  acidobacter both have a  negative predictive value with universe  of 98  oftentimes it is unlikely the  anti-pseudomonal therapy  will help in those settings particularly  extremely broad spectrum agents  like carbopenums  now when organ when identifying specific  organisms  such as e coli for example  once again knowledge of your local  resistance factors are going to be  key so in this kind of algorithmic box  here e coli is identified  as we know from an earlier slide and the  discussion with dr snyder  resistance genes are going to be  identified  by univero to the extent of what that  panel covers so ctxm  is an example of that and that's largely  related to cephalosporin resistance and  espl activity  in the setting of e coli luckily  e coli usually becomes an espl  by using the ctxm gene that is the most  common mechanism to have an espl  phenotype  so if that gene is identified it allows  you to escalate quickly to maripin if  that patient was not already on it  however if that gene is not identified  it's not to say that there's no chance  of espl but it's a very low chance  especially if you know your hospital  antibiogram particularly well  in that setting generally speaking  ceftriaxone is going to be active there  are some rare cases where plasma  mediated amp c betalactamases can be  present in e coli  but oftentimes that represents one  percent or less of isolates  so oftentimes you can de-escalate off of  anti-pseudomonal therapy  to something like ceftriaxone if e coli  is identified  so that is one of the mechanisms by  which univero can be useful and you  don't have to wait for  final phenotypic susceptibility tests to  make an adjustment  in the next example uh let's take  something like pseudomonas aeruginosa  being identified  as most of us know pseudomonas has  multiple resistance mechanisms  that are extremely diverse and are also  not  recognized by univero these include  things like amp cd repression which can  lead to resistance  with cefepime peptazo and ceftazidime  can also have porn mutations which can  lead to resistance  with carbopenum so in a setting like  this  using univero plus your local  antibiogram data and knowledge of these  resistance mechanisms can still help  guide appropriate therapy for your  pseudomonas  even without telling you what the  resistance factors are  for example idsa guidelines suggest  doing combination therapy  for half and fat for critically ill  patients  because of the likelihood of significant  resistance in some of these grab  negative organisms  if you've identified pseudomonas that  makes you feel far more comfortable  about continuing that combination  therapy even though it involves an agent  that is potentially more toxic like an  aminoglycoside knowing that  you're actually potentially getting some  benefit out of it compared to something  like e coli without any resistance  factors you may feel far more  comfortable  just doing one agent rather than come  combining with an amine glycoside  because you know that a beta-lactam  especially if you're going broad with  miropenum  is highly unlikely to miss any of those  organisms  the utilization of univero plus your  knowledge of gram-negative resistance  specifically in pseudomonas can still  help  guide appropriate therapy even if it  remains broad  in the setting of combination therapy  now that is largely the three methods by  which we utilized  univero and hopefully helpful in some  ways that you can utilize this  technology to help  de-escalate or escalate when appropriate  as we found in this evaluation the  universal lower rest  track lower respiratory tract panel can  provide rapid and actionable results  particularly if used in conjunction with  an active antimicrobial stewardship  program  the negative predictive values of  univero  can be extremely useful in reducing  excessive exposure to unnecessary  antimicrobials  particularly mrsa therapy and at times  carbopenum and anti-pseudomonal therapy  now with all that being said while  univero can be useful on its own  knowledge of resistance mechanisms of  some of these key organisms  and knowledge of your local resistance  rates  are likely going to be necessary in  combination with univero  to make the best possible decisions for  patients that you're seeing  while univer as we said can be useful on  its own really the best way to get the  most out of it is to use this  combination  approach to help guide therapy and avoid  any adverse effects  thank you very much for listening to  this we will move on to the  q a portion of this series  please if anybody has any questions let  us know and we would be happy to discuss  further  thank you  than